  "content": {
    "id": "p9-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "If one of the roots of $x^2+bx+c=0$ is twice the other root, show that $2b^2=9c$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$3\\alpha=-b$ and $2\\alpha^2=c$, so $2\\left(-\\frac{b}{3}\\right)^2=c$, giving $2b^2=9c$.",
      "worked": "Let the roots be $\\alpha$ and $2\\alpha$.\n\nSum of roots:\n$$3\\alpha=-b\\qquad(1)$$\nProduct of roots:\n$$2\\alpha^2=c\\qquad(2)$$\nRearrange (1): $\\alpha=-\\frac{b}{3}$. Substitute into (2):\n$$\\begin{aligned}2\\left(-\\frac{b}{3}\\right)^2&=c\\\\\\frac{2b^2}{9}&=c\\\\2b^2&=9c\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 42
  },
